A||||8||Greg Piet||YOU DARN V-8'ers||||12||
Z||000000||Greg Piet||02-27-2001||04:47 PM||||Went road racing for the first time, and had sooo many problems...  going into the turn to hot, boiling my DOT4 brake fluid, improper racing line, intermittant rain, using street tires,the list goes on.<P>I cant even keep my v6 on the track, let alone a V8...  so I ask myself why in the world would I need a V8?  But you folks who post about the v8 are just torturing me for the following reason...<P>The thing is that my instructor told me I NEED a faster car, so I wont feel so compelled to enter the turn too fast.<BR>I dont know if I believe that, but I know the mustang cobras passed me like I was standing still in the straightaways even if I did hit the proper line on the previous turn.<P>So I say grrrr to all you v8'ers just because I want to be in the v8 Fiero club too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">  <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!||||reg||12
Z||000001||Ed||02-27-2001||05:52 PM||||Now, now, you know better.<P>All it takes to go faster is MONEY!  Unless you win overall at Le Mans, your car is too slow.  What do you want? A Mustang?  A Corvette (they have V-8s).  A new Honda S2000 (they passed me at the driving school).  A Porsche? A Ferrari?  ETC.  <P>You know what I'm saying.  Someone's always faster.  Some car is always better.  The Porsche (my fantasy car) magazine has lots of adds for bigger brakes, engine upgrades, suspension upgrades, for the discontented.  <P>I've reconciled myself to what I've got.  My car is paid for.  I could afford to go into debt for one of the aforementioned cars, but why? Enjoy what you've got (or put in 3.4.  One of our club members did and now its faster than a Boxster, at least on the straight).  Oops, sorry to feed your need for speed.  <P>Besides, if you like road racing, you don't want to add another 200 pounds to the rear of your already tail heavy car.  Think big-block Corvette and their handling and brake problems.  <P>P.S.  One of my SCCA driving instructors wanted me to go into the corner HOTTER!  <P>P.P.S.  The Corvettes and Vipers also passed me "like I was standing still", but how much did your Fiero cost and how much does a new Corvette or Viper cost?  And the upkeep, and the insurance, and what kind of fuel consumption? <P>P.P.P.S.  And the Fiero was faster than the '81 RX7 and the BMW sedan, and kept up with the Viper on the turn. <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 02-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||02-27-2001||06:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ed:<BR><B><BR>P.S.  One of my SCCA driving instructors wanted me to go into the corner HOTTER!  <BR> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes My instructors kept telling me to take the corners hotter, and when I finally did, I wound up with 4 wheels in the air and going on a desert escapade...  Luckily only got a leak in the tread of one tire and easily fixed.<P>BTW: The only info on the Boxster I have is that it can take .89-.93g's on the skidpad...  On my yokohama S306 tires I pull around 1.0 g (+/- .02) on the skidpad after Eibach/KYB/rear sway/poly control arm bushings.  1.14 g's (+/- .01)with Hoosiers... I only pulled .83 g's on the yokos with a stock setup which from what I can gather is about what I have seen listed.<P>What would a typical Boxter pull with such goodies?<P>I know there are always faster cars...  I just wanna gripe a bit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>When I was not spinning off course, I was passing a late 90's vette and a first gen Rx7 (I think ITA, but definately in CSP for SOLO2)<P>I also got held up by a stock Mister two turbo on the turns, but got spanked on the straights.<P>The thing that really concerned me was my brakes...  Got those X drilled ones from the Fiero Store...  I think they are a waste!  And I didnt even KNOW there was DOT 5 brake fluid!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||02-27-2001||08:21 PM||||Don't worry about more power , get the car handling right, Then worry about power. IT's all about average speed. I can remember track racing my 86 GT when it was stock , not fun to do , it's a handful. Now the car is a joy to drive , doesn't take as much effort , used to feel like a wrestling match driving around a track , now I can plant the car on every apex. The problem isn't the cars power , it's the suspension or lack their of...<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||02-27-2001||08:42 PM||||Unless you have a lot of time and probably money- DOT 5 brake fluid will hurt you! It is not compatable with other fluids, so the entire system must be cleaned. Also, it has a little more "compression" than regular brake fluid, so the brakes may not feel as firm under the heat of competition.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||02-27-2001||10:47 PM||||Your worried about Handling and you want a V8?! ||||reg||5
